    15	**ABSTRACT**
    16	
    17	
    18	Software build systems specify how source code is transformed
    19	into deliverables. Keeping build systems in sync with the software
    20	artifacts that they build while retaining their capacity to quickly
    21	produce updated deliverables requires a serious investment of development effort. Enticed by advanced features, several software
    22	teams have migrated their build systems to a modern generation
    23	of build technologies (e.g., Bazel, Buck), which aim to reduce the
    24	maintenance and execution overhead that build systems impose
    25	on development. However, not all migrations lead to perceived
    26	improvements, ultimately culminating in abandonment of the build
    27	technology. While prior work has focused on upward migration
    28	towards more advanced technologies, so-called downgrades, i.e.,
    29	abandonment of a modern build technology in favour of a traditional one, remains largely unexplored.
    30	In this paper, we perform an empirical study to better understand the abandonment of Bazel—a modern build technology with
    31	native support for multi-language software projects and (local/distributed) artifact caching. Our investigation of 542 projects that
    32	adopt Bazel reveals that (1) 61 projects (11.2%) have abandoned
    33	Bazel; and (2) abandonment tends to occur after investing in Bazel
    34	for a substantial amount of time (a median of 638 days). Thematic
