    85	**1** **INTRODUCTION**
    86	
    87	
    88	Build systems automate the process of transforming source code
    89	into deliverables by invoking tools (e.g., compilers, testing harnesses, code generators) in a dependency-based and configurationdependent manner. Build systems are used to automate builds in
    90	development environments, e.g., within IDEs or on the CLI, and can
    91	even be used in deployment environments, e.g., where users invoke
    92	the build system to install open-source systems that are compiled
    93	from source. An effective build system helps to manage risk in
    94	software development by helping developers to detect compilation
    95	and integration problems early in the development cycle [12].
    96	Maintaining a fast and robust build system requires a continuous
    97	investment of maintenance effort to stay in sync with the other software artifacts [ 32 ]. When software projects exceed the complexity
    98	that a developer can grasp, code changes can impact unexpected
    99	parts of the system, which lead to inconsistencies during the build
   100	and at runtime [ 28 ]. Moreover, as projects age, their build processes
   101	tend to slow down [ 26, 27 ]. Since build speed directly impacts the
   102	pace at which developers can test their changes, slow builds are a
   103	common complaint among developers [29].
   104	In response to the maintenance and execution overhead of build
   105	systems, the software community has created modern build technologies, such as Bazel [1] and Buck. [2] Such build technologies provide native support for multi-language software projects, which
   106	are known to be problematic [ 36 ], and support local/distributed
   107	artifact caches to accelerate individual/team builds [13].
   108	Prior work also shows that migrating to modern build technologies can be achieved with promising results [ 9, 30, 45, 47 ];
   109	however, not all migrations lead to perceived improvements. Indeed, framework-driven build technologies (e.g., Maven) tend to
   110	be more tightly coupled to source code than more traditional technologies (e.g., Make, CMake) [ 31 ]. Moreover, framework-driven
   111	build technologies are often more prone to cloning-related issues
   112	than the more traditional technologies. This stems from strict version compatibility requirements leading to version conflicts and
   113	limited customization options. In contrast, the more traditional
   114	technologies offer a greater degree of control and flexibility, which
   115	contributes to their lower proneness to cloning-related pitfalls [ 33 ].
   116	
   117	
   118	1 [https://bazel.build/](https://bazel.build/)
   119	2 [https://buck.build/](https://buck.build/)
   120	
