   122	ICSE ’24, April 14–20, 2024, Lisbon, Portugal Mahmoud Alfadel and Shane McIntosh
   123	
   124	
   125	
   126	Upward migrations that are not perceived improvements may
   127	influence stakeholders to abandon feature-rich build technologies.
   128	For example, within the kubernetes/kubernetes project, [3] the
   129	complexity and maintenance overhead of their Bazel build system
   130	led the community to abandon Bazel in favour of Go Build—a less
   131	feature-rich build technology. A similar event within the istio/istio
   132	project prompted a lively discussion about abandoning Bazel. [4]
   133	
   134	Understanding why communities choose to migrate away from
   135	modern build systems can help stakeholders to adopt these build
   136	systems with realistic expectations and in a fashion that suits their
   137	needs. It can also help researchers and tool developers to focus on
   138	the most relevant barriers to adoption within these build systems.
   139	Hence, in this paper, we set out to study the prevalence of and rationale for downgrades of build technology. We conduct an empirical
   140	study of 542 open-source projects that adopt Bazel—one of the most
   141	(if not _the_ most) popular modern build technologies available.
   142	**Prevalence (Section 3** ). First, we study the extent to which
   143	open-source projects that adopt Bazel end up abandoning it. We
   144	observe that 61 of the 542 studied projects (11.2%) that adopted Bazel
   145	abandoned it. The abandonment occurred in the studied projects
   146	despite maintaining Bazel specifications for a median of 638 days.
   147	**Rationale (Section 4** ). Next, to understand the reasons that led
   148	adopters to abandon Bazel, we conduct a thematic analysis on 212
   149	records of commits, issue reports, and pull requests that document
   150	the final removal of Bazel specifications in the 61 projects that
   151	abandoned Bazel. We identify seven emergent themes, which span
   152	from technical challenges, such as the complexity of the build specifications and their maintenance, to a (perceived) lack of platform
   153	integration and interoperability, team coordination and onboarding
   154	challenges, and the influence of community trends.
   155	**Replacements (Section 5** ). Then, we analyze the build technologies that the 61 projects adopted after abandoning Bazel, observing
   156	a broad set of common replacements, including language-specific
   157	tools, such as Go Build, as well as more conventional options like
   158	CMake and even low-level build technologies like pure Make. These
   159	replacements are often less feature-rich, but more familiar to their
   160	community members, simplifying their maintenance.
   161	**Generalizability (Section 6** ). Finally, we explore the analytic
   162	generalizability of our themes by conducting a confirmatory study
   163	of a second downgrade case (from Gradle to other build technologies) and a contradictory upgrade case (from other build technologies to Bazel). We find that five of the seven emergent themes from
   164	the Bazel downgrade context also appear in the Gradle downgrade
   165	context, and that only one of the themes (the influence of community trends) appears in the contradictory Bazel upgrade context.
   166	
   167	
   168	**Contributions.** This paper contributes: **(1)** _empirically grounded_
   169	_estimates_ of the prevalence of build technology downgrades; **(2)** a
   170	_catalogue of seven emergent themes_ that explain why 61 projects have
   171	downgraded their build systems; **(3)** a _list of popular replacement_
   172	_technologies_ while shedding light on key factors that influence their
   173	adoption; and **(4)** _a checklist_ that stakeholders can consult before
   174	adopting modern build technologies. To foster future work, we
   175	make our replication package publicly available. [5]
