   184	**2** **STUDY DESIGN**
   185	
   186	
   187	In this section, we provide an overview of the most common build
   188	technologies (Section 2.1). Then, we describe our rationale for focusing on the Bazel build technology (Section 2.2) and explain our
   189	approach to project selection (Section 2.3).
   190	
   191	
   192	**2.1** **Existing Build Technologies**
   193	
   194	Dozens of build technologies are available for communities to use. [6]
   195	
   196	Broadly speaking, these technologies have converged on various
   197	design paradigms [30, 42, 43]. The four most common ones are:
   198	
   199	
   200	  - _Low-level_ technologies that require explicitly defined build
   201	dependencies (which may also be pattern-based or inferred)
   202	between each input and output file (e.g., Make [14]).
   203	
   204	  - _Abstraction-based_ technologies that use high-level project
   205	information, such as the project name and the list of files to
   206	build, to generate low-level specifications (e.g., CMake).
   207	
   208	  - _Framework-driven_ technologies that reduce “boilerplate” dependency expressions that are typical of low-level technologies in favour of conventions by expecting that, e.g., input
   209	and output files appear in predefined locations (e.g., Maven).
   210	
   211	  - _Modern_ build technologies provide native support for multilanguage software projects and support local/distributed
   212	artifact caches to accelerate individual/team builds [ 13 ]. Such
   213	build technologies differ from traditional systems by using
   214	fine-grained dependency management, monorepo support,
   215	and integrated static analysis (e.g., Bazel).
   216	
   217	
   218	**2.2** **Studied Build Technology**
   219	
   220	
   221	To counteract increases in project complexity, organizationallyscaling build technologies have been created to build, test, and
   222	package large amounts of cross-language code simultaneously. In
   223	contrast to prior build technologies (e.g., Make, CMake, Maven),
   224	these modern ones use more abstraction for targets and sources,
   225	and provide native support for producing deliverables of common
   226	types, such as libraries, binaries, scripts, and datasets.
   227	These modern technologies also natively support projects that
   228	are implemented in multiple languages, which makes them an attractive choice for managing the build process of so-called “monorepos” [ 34 ], i.e., where several related projects are versioned within
   229	a single repository. The entirety of the dependency graph is computed, inferred, and tracked to parallelize work, cache results, and
   230	mitigate non-determinism in build behaviour.
   231	In our study, we choose to focus on the Bazel technology due
   232	to its popularity among modern build technologies. For example,
   233	compared to Buck and Pants (i.e., its closest competitors), Bazel
   234	is gaining more traction in the StackOverflow community, [7] with
   235	3,229 questions posted (as of December 2023), whereas only 112
   236	and 48 questions are tagged with Buck and Pants, respectively.
   237	Furthermore, our preliminary analysis in Section 3 reveals that the
   238	number of projects that have adopted Bazel exceeds the combined
   239	count of projects that have adopted Buck and Pants by a substantial
   240	margin, i.e., the number of projects adopting Bazel, Buck, and Pants
   241	in the World of Code corpus [24] is 542, 8, and 7, respectively.
   242	
   243	
   244	6 [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_build_automation_software](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_build_automation_software)
   245	7 [https://stackoverflow.com/](https://stackoverflow.com/)
   246	
   247	
   248	The Classics Never Go Out of Style ICSE ’24, April 14–20, 2024, Lisbon, Portugal
   249	
   250	
   251	
   252	**Table 1: Typical build technology paradigms and supported**
   253	**features (adapted from McIntosh et al. [30, 32]).**
