   303	**Figure 1: An overview of our data filtering approach.**
   304	
   305	
   306	Bazel is an open-source, declarative build system that was initially released by Google in 2015. Bazel is fast because it only rebuilds what is needed, which is achieved by its built-in support for
   307	local and distributed caching of both build artifacts and tests, along
   308	with an optimized dependency analysis. [1] Moreover, although Bazel
   309	already supports a plethora of platforms and languages, it can be
   310	extended to support other frameworks and languages.
   311	
   312	
   313	**2.3** **Candidate Projects**
   314	
   315	
   316	Our goal is to study abandonment of the Bazel build technology.
   317	To perform our study, we need to collect a dataset of projects that
   318	have, at one point, adopted Bazel. It is important that we study a
   319	large sample of software projects in order to improve confidence
   320	in the conclusions that we draw. Hence, we analyze the large corpus of open-source version history collected in the World of Code
   321	(WoC) [ 24 ]. The WoC corpus is updated on a monthly basis and
   322	contains over 18 billion Git objects. We begin our analysis by querying the WoC corpus for projects that are hosted on GitHub. This
   323	query returns 7,731,242 public GitHub repositories.
   324	Since GitHub hosts projects that are not yet mature or of sufficient complexity to warrant analysis, we apply three filters to select
   325	projects that are appropriate for our study. Figure 1 provides an
   326	overview of those filters, each of which we describe below.
   327	
   328	
   329	**DF1: Select non-forked projects.** Forking a repository allows
   330	developers to experiment with changes without affecting the original project. We remove forks to reduce the quantity of duplicated
   331	project history, which can sk<response clipped><NOTE>To save on context only part of this file has been shown to you. You should retry this tool after you have searched inside the file with `grep -n` in order to find the line numbers of what you are looking for.</NOTE>


